## Mandatory patterns

### Mutex

<% if (mobileFramework === 'expo-rn-nativewind') { -%>
All SQLite writes go through `executeWithMutex()` (serial queue) to avoid SQLITE_BUSY. Nested calls bypass the queue (inline) to avoid deadlock. **Never perform direct writes outside the mutex.**
<% } else if (mobileFramework === 'flutter') { -%>
All SQLite writes go through a mutex/lock mechanism (serial queue) to avoid SQLITE_BUSY. Nested calls bypass the queue (inline) to avoid deadlock. **Never perform direct writes outside the mutex.**
<% } -%>

### Local migrations

<% if (mobileFramework === 'expo-rn-nativewind') { -%>
New columns/tables in local SQLite are added via incremental `ALTER TABLE` in the base offline service — **not via Alembic or backend migrations** (those are only for the backend PostgreSQL).
<% } else if (mobileFramework === 'flutter') { -%>
New columns/tables in local SQLite are added via incremental `ALTER TABLE` in the database helper or via drift schema versioning — **not via backend migrations** (those are only for the backend PostgreSQL).
<% } -%>

### Purge guards

A `*_offline` parent row is only purged if ALL child relations are also `synced`. Any child with `pending` status blocks the parent purge indefinitely.

### Dead-letter queue

Rows with `status = 'dead'` (5+ attempts) are not retried automatically. Data can be silently lost. When adding new entities, consider dead-letter handling.

